# mocari 0.4.1 cannot fade a motion: it drops the fade values the model files carry
# and hard-codes the motion-level fade to zero, so every clip starts with a jump and a
# replaced clip disappears in one frame.
#
# Cubism lets a motion declare its fade in one of two places, and the test corpus uses
# both:
#
#   model3.json   FileReferences.Motions.<group>[i].FadeInTime / FadeOutTime
#                 (Haru, Hiyori and Mark declare 0.5 s here)
#   motion3.json  Meta.FadeInTime / FadeOutTime
#                 (Mao declares 0.0 / 0.25 / 0.5 / 1.0 per clip, Ren 0.25 s on two)
#
# where neither declares one, the SDK uses 1 s. A declared model3.json value wins when
# it is present and >= 0 (CubismUserModel::LoadMotion), so it cannot be read from the
# motion file alone - and a declared 0.0 is a real value, not an absent one.
#
# What 0.4.1 does with that data:
#
#   - json/model3.rs   MotionReference keeps only `File`; both fade keys are parsed
#                      away, so the value most of the corpus actually uses is
#                      unreachable.
#   - json/motion3.rs  MotionMeta drops Meta.FadeInTime / FadeOutTime for the same
#                      reason, and parameter_curve_fade_weight returns the player
#                      weight alone as soon as a curve declares no fade of its own -
#                      which is most curves. Even a motion-level fade would have been
#                      ignored; the reference multiplies weight, fade-in and fade-out.
#   - motion.rs        MotionPlayer::apply passes 0.0 as the motion-level fade, so
#                      parameter_curve_fade_weight sees 1.0 for every curve that does
#                      not declare its own fade - no fade in, no fade out.
#
# The fix keeps the two declarations apart (the render layer composes them with its own
# default and a host override, so one place decides the precedence) and gives the player
# explicit fade times plus a forced fade-out:
#
#   MotionReference::fade_in_time() / fade_out_time()   Option<f32>, negative = unset
#   MotionMeta::fade_in_time() / fade_out_time()        same
#   MotionPlayer::with_fade(motion, fade_in, fade_out)  values that win over the file
#   MotionPlayer::fade_out() -> bool                    fade out from now, over this
#                                                       player's own fade-out, clamped
#                                                       to the clip's remaining time
#
# `Eq` is dropped from Model3 / RawModel3 / FileReferences / MotionReference because they
# now carry f32s; nothing in the crate requires it.
#
# Apply with:
#   git apply -p1 <this file>    # on a copy of the crates.io 0.4.1 sources
#
# Verified by the unit tests carried in the patch (the three precedence levels, a
# declared zero, the fade-out countdown and its clamp to a short clip, curve fade
# weights, and parsing both files), and by rendering .refs/models/Hiyori headlessly
# through live2d-render, whose model3.json declares 0.5 s while its motion files
# declare nothing: one step into an Idle clip now lands 0.05/255 away from the rest
# pose instead of 1.57/255 with the fade cut.
#
# Worth reporting upstream: all of it is local, and a forced fade-out belongs in
# MotionPlayer rather than in every consumer.
